# .clinerules — Phishing Email Analyser

This file summarises the non-negotiable rules for anyone (human or AI) working
in this repo. Full detail lives in the original project brief; this is the
quick-reference so nothing drifts silently over time.

## Working agreement
- Explain every important decision in plain English before implementing it.
  Record it in docs/decisions/NNN-title.md (Context, Options, Decision,
  Trade-off), under 250 words.
- Work phase by phase (see project Section 15). Stop at the end of each
  phase, summarise, wait for approval before continuing.
- Never add a dependency without asking first. State: what it does, why not
  write it ourselves, its size, licence, maintenance status.
- Prefer boring, readable code over clever code. Comment the WHY of a rule,
  not the WHAT.
- LEARNER-OWNED modules (message/header parser, URL analyser, scoring
  engine): in scaffold mode, deliver only interfaces + design notes +
  documented edge cases + a failing test suite. Do not implement the logic.
  Wait for the human's implementation, then review critically.

## Hard constraints (privacy)
- All analysis runs in the browser. Email content is never sent anywhere.
- No analytics, telemetry, error reporting, remote fonts, CDNs, remote
  images, or external scripts. Self-host everything.
- No localStorage/sessionStorage/IndexedDB/cookies for email content. Only a
  theme preference may persist. Provide a "Clear" button.
- Never put input into the URL (query or fragment).
- No service worker in v1.

## Hard constraints (app security)
- Never use innerHTML with user-derived content. Use textContent / DOM node
  creation. Parse pasted HTML only via DOMParser into an inert document.
- Never render an extracted URL as a clickable link. Always plain text, with
  a "copy defanged" option.
- CSP via <meta> + a _headers file (frame-ancestors only works as a header).

## Technology
- TypeScript + Vite + Vitest + Playwright (dev only) + ESLint.
- Zero shipped runtime dependencies, with two possible exceptions decided via
  decision records: jsQR (QR decode) and tldts (public suffix list).
- Plain CSS only. No React, no backend, no database, no API keys.
- src/core/* is pure functions, no DOM access, testable in Node.
